{"id":1235,"date":"2020-11-24T13:15:13","date_gmt":"2020-11-24T21:15:13","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1235"},"modified":"2024-09-01T20:28:55","modified_gmt":"2024-09-02T03:28:55","slug":"codrone-arduino-return-functions","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-return-functions\/","title":{"rendered":"Return Functions"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215dd94b3c\" name=\"6a2215dd94b3c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Return functions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You&#8217;ve already learned about functions &#8212; they&#8217;re blocks of code that can be named and reused in different parts of your program. Return functions also do this, but they also return a value to your program. One real-life example is turning in your homework. The input to the function would be your answers to the homework. The function would calculate the number of correct answers and return that value.<\/p>\n<p>This is what a return function usually looks like:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">ReturnDataType FunctionName(inputs){ \r\n    statements;\r\n    return\u00a0ReturnValue;\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>This is what&#8217;s going on here:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>ReturnDataType is the type of data that will be returned. If you don&#8217;t remember the data types, check the Variables lesson.<\/li>\n<li>FunctionName is the name of the function. This can be whatever you want!<\/li>\n<li>Inputs are anything that the function needs to run. This could be a duration of time, the LEDs that should light up, the drone to connect to, or in some cases, like with <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">CoDrone.move()<\/code>, nothing at all.<\/li>\n<li>Statements are the code that will run when the function is called.<\/li>\n<li><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">return<\/code> is the command that will exit out of the function and return the value to you.<\/li>\n<li>ReturnValue is the value that will be returned to you in the program. Make sure it matches up with the data type you wrote in ReturnDataType!<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>So if you were to write a function that would return the number of correct answers, this is what it could look like:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"csharp\">int getHomeworkGrade(int answers){\r\n    count number of correct answers\r\n    return number of correct answers  \r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215dd94c4d\" name=\"6a2215dd94c4d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Writing a return function<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Ready to write your own return function? Try one that will do math for you! This function will accept two whole numbers, multiply them together, and return the answer:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">int Mult(int x, int y){\r\n    int z = x * y; \r\n    return z;\r\n}<\/pre>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p>The key word for this function is <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">return<\/code>. That indicates that the function will end and that the value of the variable you chose will be returned.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215dd94cc3\" name=\"6a2215dd94cc3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Random return function<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Here are some examples of other functions that return values.<\/p>\n<p><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">max()<\/code> takes two numbers as input and returns the larger of the two. In this case, since 100 is larger than 20, 100 would be returned.<\/p>\n<div class=\"CodeMirror notranslate cm-s-default\">\n<div>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">int Max(int x, int y){\r\n  if (x &gt; y) {\r\n    return x;\r\n  }\r\n  else {\r\n    return y;\r\n  }\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>Now try to write a function that takes two numbers as input, but returns the smaller of the two. Your code might look like this:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">int Min(int x, int y){\r\n    if (x &lt; y) {\r\n        return x;\r\n    }\r\n    else {\r\n        return y;\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n\r\nint MinNum = Min(100,20);<\/pre>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215dd94d32\" name=\"6a2215dd94d32\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Calling return functions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Calling a return function is a little different than calling a void function (and if you forgot what a void function looks like, go back to the lesson Void Functions). Instead of writing a function and then using its name in your program, the setup looks similar to how you would declare a variable. This is because the return value needs to be stored. Make sure the data type of your variable matches the return data type of the function!<\/p>\n<p>For example, if you wrote a function to read the value of your joystick (anywhere from 0 to 1023) and return it, this is what it would look like:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">int x = analogRead(23);<\/pre>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p>The value of <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">analogRead(23)<\/code> will be stored in variable x.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"activity2__spacer\">\n<div class=\"activity2__question\">\n<div class=\"styled-checkbox activity2__button-checkbox\"><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215dd94d99\" name=\"6a2215dd94d99\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Programming example: Part 1<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]For this lesson, you&#8217;re going to write a program to have your CoDrone&#8217;s arms change color while it spins! The beginning of the program should include the library.<\/p>\n<p>Above void setup, you need to write a function definition that will return a random number (we called ours <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">RandomNum<\/code>). In that function, initialize a variable for time, and assign it <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">millis()<\/code><code class=\"code-snippet-inline\"><\/code>multiplied by a thousand. Refer back to the Timers lesson if you need a refresher on <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">millis()<\/code>. Next, have the function return the time plus 1 in percent form.<\/p>\n<p>Finally, include the connection and takeoff in void setup.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt;\r\n\r\nint RandomNum(int x){\r\n    int time = int(millis() * 1000);\r\n    return time % (x + 1);\r\n  }\r\n  \r\nvoid setup(){\r\n    CoDrone.begin(115200); \r\n    CoDrone.pair();\r\n    CoDrone.takeoff();\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215dd94df1\" name=\"6a2215dd94df1\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Programming example: Part 2<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In void loop, set pin 11 as a sensor. Write your program so that if IR sensor 11 is triggered, your CoDrone will stop. Else, your CoDrone will yaw to the right with the arms flickering a random color.<\/p>\n<p>Wondering why there&#8217;s three <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">RandomNum<\/code> return functions in a row? You want a random value to be returned for the R, G, and B values of the LED.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">void loop() {\r\n  \r\n  byte bt1 = digitalRead(11);\r\n\r\n  if (bt1) {\r\n     CoDrone.emergencyStop();\r\n  \r\n  }\r\n  else if (!bt1){\r\n    CoDrone.setYaw(50);\r\n    CoDrone.setArmLED(RandomNum(255),RandomNum(255),RandomNum(255), BLINK, 50);\r\n  }\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215dd94e48\" name=\"6a2215dd94e48\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Your final code should look like this:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt;\r\n\r\n  int RandomNum(int x){\r\n  int time = int(millis() * 1000);\r\n  return time % (x + 1);\r\n  }\r\n  \r\nvoid setup(){\r\n  CoDrone.begin(115200); \r\n  CoDrone.pair();\r\n\r\n  CoDrone.takeoff();\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid loop() {\r\n  \r\n  byte bt1 = digitalRead(11);\r\n\r\n  if (bt1) {\r\n     CoDrone.emergencyStop();\r\n  \r\n  }\r\n  else if (!bt1){\r\n    CoDrone.setYaw(50);\r\n    CoDrone.setArmLED(RandomNum(255),RandomNum(255),RandomNum(255), BLINK, 50);\r\n  }\r\n}<\/pre>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215dd94ea7\" name=\"6a2215dd94ea7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<strong>Challenge: Wandering CoDrone<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Create a program that will make your CoDrone fly in random directions at random speeds!<\/p>\n<p><strong>Rules:<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Make sure your location has a lot of room to fly in multiple directions.<\/li>\n<li>Use return functions to return your random numbers.<\/li>\n<li>Do not go up to 100 on your throttle!<\/li>\n<li>Make sure you include a kill switch!<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 9 Return functions [vc_column_text]You've already learned about functions --- they're blocks of code that can be named and reused in different parts of your program. Return functions also&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-return-functions\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1749,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":804,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1737,1746,1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,1392,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2195,2163,2164,2165,2131,2122,2206],"class_list":["post-1235","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-advanced","lesson-tag-arduino","lesson-tag-codrone","lesson-tag-codrone-pro","lesson-tag-functions","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-return-functions","module-advanced","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1235","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":2,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1235\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":2477,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1235\/revisions\/2477"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1749"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1235"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=1235"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=1235"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}